From e4927cd97467ceaf96a27d2c6a5577d2a393bd1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marlos001 <marlos.guedes@sou.inteli.edu.br>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 11:32:33 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] power: supply: rk817-charger: make the USB input current
 limit writable

The driver fixes the USB input current limit at 1.5A, at init and after
every unplug, and offers no way to change it, so users cannot select a
higher input ceiling. Actual current can still be constrained by the
unchanged 4.5V input-voltage floor.

Expose the limit as the charger's input_current_limit, readable and
writable in microamps. Reads report what the PMIC is set to. A write
selects the highest of the PMIC's limits (80mA, 450mA, 850mA, 1.5A,
1.75A and 2A) that does not exceed the request, so requests above 2A
select 2A; requests below 80mA are rejected. The 2.5A and 3A settings
are not offered: the driver cannot tell what the source supplies, and
the datasheet gives the 3A setting a 2.8A to 3.2A tolerance.

The default stays 1.5A, and anything higher has to be asked for again
for each charger:

- A write fails with -ENODEV when no charger is attached. The plug
  state is checked under a mutex that the unplug handler also takes to
  restore the default, so that restore always follows any write it
  races with.
- Every plug-in retries the default reset, including after a failed
  unplug reset.
- The default is also restored at suspend, at shutdown, when probe
  fails and when the driver is unbound, after its power supplies are
  unregistered.
- Limits above the default fail with -EOPNOTSUPP unless thermal
  foldback is enabled and the battery ceilings are at most 2A/4.35V.
- A failed default reset aborts probe or suspend rather than carrying
  an unconfirmed input limit into operation or sleep.
- If writing a limit fails, the default is written back.

Take an error-checked suspend snapshot and commit it only after all
register accesses succeed; do not reuse unchecked gauge refresh values.

The minimum input voltage is unchanged at 4.5V and is now written
together with the current limit.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
